|~ `::frame`, the kind of one level of the path a failure flew, and what
   is declared under it: the refusal of a payload that holds no subject
   and steps, and the words that read a frame.
   The decisions it exists for: [QSERR#trail]. ~|

:frame
  |~~ A frame is one level of the path a failure flew, the step that broke
      there with the value it met: the subject of the step, the steps of
      its level from the one that broke, and, where the step ran its code
      on a part of its subject, the path of that part.
      `::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)]` is `add 1`, which broke on `"x"`,
      and `mul 2`, which did not run; `::frame[[1 "x" 3] ~(map ~(mul 2)) /1]`
      is `map`, which broke where its code ran on the element at `/1`.

      A trail holds a frame for each step that raised a failure or handed
      it on, a frame a line, and a frame prints on one line whatever its
      subject holds. `subject`, `step`, `skipped` and `place` read
      a frame, and a path reads it as the vector it is, `/0` its subject,
      so `!| get /trail/1/0/1` reads the element the code of `map` broke
      on. The steps skipped at the level that reads a failure run again on
      another value by `apply`.
      ::snippet~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | last)
      ::link~(::trail | doc) ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps |~~ The subject, the steps and the place. ~~| ::vec
    | holds :PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError
        |~~ A frame holds the subject a step met and the quote of the
            steps from the one that broke, one step at least, and after
            them the path of the part of the subject the step ran its code
            on, where it ran it on one: `::frame["x" ~(add 1)]`,
            `::frame[[1 "x"] ~(map ~(mul 2)) /1]`.
        ~~|
        ~(allOf ~(count | gte 2) ~(count | lte 3) ~(get /1 | is ::quote) ~(get /1 | count | gt 0) ~(anyOf ~(count | eq 2) ~(get /2 | is ::path)))
    | proves :holdsTheStepThatBrokeAndTheValueItMet
        |~~ The frame of `add 1` holds the value it met. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trail | first | eq ::frame["x" ~(add 1)])
    | proves :holdsThePlaceOfThePart
        |~~ The frame of `map` holds the place of the element its code
            broke on.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | last | eq ::frame[[1 "x" 3] ~(map ~(mul 2)) /1])
    | proves :printsOnOneLine
        |~~ A frame prints on one line whatever its subject holds. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b [1 2]}} | get /c !| trail | first | print | lines | count | eq 1)
    | proves :PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError/refusesASubjectAlone
        |~~ A subject with no steps is no frame. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x"] !| type | eq ::frame/PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError)
    | proves :PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError/refusesAPlaceThatIsNoPath
        |~~ A place that is no path is refused. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x" ~(add 1) 1] !| type | eq ::frame/PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map is no frame. ~~|
        ~(::frame{:step ~(add 1)} !| type | eq ::frame/PayloadKindNotTakenError)

:frame/subject
  |~~ Answers the subject of the frame, the value the step that broke
      there met, `::frame["x" ~(add 1)] | subject` answering `"x"`: the
      first frame of a trail holds the value the refusal met, and the
      frame of a word that ran its code on a part of its subject the whole
      subject, its `place` naming the part.
      ::link~(::frame/place | doc) ::link~(::frame/step | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The value the step met, of any kind. ~~| ::any
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `subject` reads a frame, which the trail of a failure holds,
            `"x" | add 1 !| trail | first | subject`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheValueTheStepMet
        |~~ The first frame holds the value the refusal met. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trail | first | subject | eq "x")
    | proves :answersTheWholeSubjectOfAWord
        |~~ The frame of `map` holds the vector it ran its code over. ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | last | subject | eq [1 "x" 3])
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesAnError
        |~~ An error at rest is read by `trail` first. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| subject !| type | eq ::frame/subject/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(get /0)

:frame/step
  |~~ Answers the step that broke, the quote of its one step,
      `::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)] | step` answering `~(add 1)`, so
      `!| trail | map ~step` reads the path of a failure a step at a time,
      from the step that refused to the step of the level that reads it.
      ::link~(::frame/skipped | doc) ::link~(::trail | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The step that broke, a quote of its one step. ~~| ::quote
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `step` reads a frame, which the trail of a failure holds,
            `"x" | add 1 !| trail | first | step`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheStepThatBroke
        |~~ The step that broke is the first of the steps of the frame. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)] | step | eq ~(add 1))
    | proves :readsAPathAStepAtATime
        |~~ The steps of a trail read from where the failure arose outward.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | map ~step | eq [~(mul 2) ~(map ~(mul 2))])
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesAQuote
        |~~ A quote is no frame. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | step !| type | eq ::frame/step/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(get /1 | take 1)

:frame/skipped
  |~~ Answers the steps the failure skipped after the step that broke, a
      quote, the empty quote where it skipped none:
      `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail | last | skipped` answers `~(mul 2)`,
      the steps left at the level that reads the failure, which run again
      on another value by `apply`.
      ::link~(::frame/step | doc) ::link~(::any/apply | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The steps skipped, a quote. ~~| ::quote
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `skipped` reads a frame, which the trail of a failure holds,
            `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail | last | skipped`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheStepsSkipped
        |~~ The steps after the one that broke did not run. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail | last | skipped | eq ~(mul 2))
    | proves :answersTheEmptyQuoteForNone
        |~~ A step that skipped none answers the empty quote. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trail | last | skipped | eq ~())
    | proves :runsAgainByApply
        |~~ The steps skipped run again on another value. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail | last | skipped | :rest it | 5 | apply rest | eq 10)
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector is no frame. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | skipped !| type | eq ::frame/skipped/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(get /1 | drop 1)

:frame/place
  |~~ Answers the path of the part of the subject the step ran its code on
      where it broke, null where it ran it on no part:
      `[1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | last | place` answers `/1`. A
      word that runs its code on the elements of its subject, `map`,
      `filter`, `every`, `any` and `reduce`, and the keys of `sort`,
      `min`, `max`, `groupBy` and `indexBy`, names the place of the
      element, its index or its key, and `update` the path it wrote; the
      subject reads the part by it, `get (f | place)` with `f` the frame.
      ::link~(::frame/subject | doc) ::link~(::path | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The path of the part, null where the step ran its code on none. ~~| #[::null ::path]
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `place` reads a frame, which the trail of a failure holds,
            `[1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | last | place`.
        ~~|
    | proves :namesTheElementTheCodeBrokeOn
        |~~ `map` names the index of the element its code broke on. ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | last | place | eq /1)
    | proves :namesTheKeyOfAMap
        |~~ Over a map it names the key. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b "x"} | map ~(add 1) !| trail | last | place | eq /b)
    | proves :namesThePathUpdateWrote
        |~~ `update` names the path it wrote. ~~|
        ~({:a {:n "x"}} | update /a/n ~(add 1) !| trail | last | place | eq /a/n)
    | proves :answersNullWhereNoCodeRanOnAPart
        |~~ A step that ran no code on a part names none. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trail | first | place | eq null)
    | proves :readsThePartFromTheSubject
        |~~ The subject of the frame reads the part by its place. ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | last | :f it | subject | get (f | place) | eq "x")
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no frame. ~~|
        ~(5 | place !| type | eq ::frame/place/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(query /2)
